Have you ever wanted to do something for Linux, but don't know how to
program? Well, here's your chance!

I'm looking for a great many volunteers to write for the _Linux User's
Guide_, part of the Linux Documentation Project's many efforts. Currently,
I'd like people to volunteer who are willing to write a chapter of this
book.

The _Linux User's Guide_ (LUG) is intended for new users of the Unix
operating system.  The targeted audience are people who have just installed
Linux, but have no idea what to do with it. It will hopefully be useful for
both single user machines, and any Linux machines that support more than
one simultaneous users.

If you're interested, the following is itching to be written:

- A chapter on the interesting things you can do with "simple" commands
like ls, rm, cp, mv, grep, more, cat, and so on. Interesting applications
of these commands would be nice. An introduction to a more complicated idea
like awk, sed, and/or shell programming could also be done. (Note that this
could turn into several chapters: I'm flexible!)
- A chapter on the X Window System, using twm. Anything falling under this
is welcome.
- A chapter on multiple user utilities, like mail, news, talk, finger, and
other (plain user) things for communicating with other users. Not
configuration, though.
- A chapter on commands on larger Unix utilities that an ex-DOS user might
be interested in, such as kermit.
- Anything else that you can think of! (Except emacs. That's being
covered.) Suggestions are welcomed and encouraged.

The work is being done in LaTeX using linuxdoc.sty. (If you really, really
hate LaTeX and/or can't learn it, I'll accept things in plain ASCII.)  Join
the DOC channel to learn more about the general ideas behind the LDP.

If you're interested in doing one of the above chapters or just have
comments or wishes (my ideal Unix book would include...) please mail
greenfie@gauss.rutgers.edu. I'll tell you if someone has already
volunteered to do a section and mail any writing guidelines that should be
followed.

Larry Greenfield
