For all of you who are looking for a Pascal or Modula-2 compiler on
Linux; and of course for all who already know Ada. 

Here comes the Gnu Ada compiler for Linux in its newly updated form as
of October 1st (V1.53). The compiler still is under heavy development. A
more or less final version is announced for the end of the year.

The binary distribution can be found at the following places:

sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/development/ada/gnat-931001.tar.gz
nic.funet.fi:/pub/OS/Linux/util/languages/gnat-931001.tar.gz
tsx-11.mit.edu: ??? stil in the incoming directory

The GNAT project will implement the full Ada9X language which has full
support of Object Oriented Programming (using a slightly different
approach than C++, see the paper intro-9x.txt), has already working
Generics (aka templates), exception handling, and a full tasking support
within the language itself (the runtime support is not yet ready)

There still are some problems in the current version (and in my Linux
port). If you find errors in the already implemented parts (see below),
please write to gnat-report@cs.nyu.edu. If you think it is Linux
related, please tell me (ebert@enpc.fr). I have tested the compiler on
the examples and of course on itself. The executable compiler that you
find in this distribution has compiled itself.



This package contains essentially three files:

./usr/bin/gcc
./usr/bin/gnatbind
./usr/lib/gcc-lib/i386-linux/2.4.5/gnat1

and some stuff in
 ./usr/src/gnat

The first one is a patched driver program for gcc (2.4.5) in order to
choose the GNAT compiler when it encounters source files ending in
".ada", ".ads", and ".adb". (Don't worry, it still works for C/C++/Obj-C)


Documentation can be found in the directory

./usr/bin/src/gnat/doc


Perhaps it is a good idea to get the latest Ada9X reference manual from:

ajpo.sei.cmu.edu:/public/ada9x/rm9x/v4.0

It might look a bit thick, but there are a lot of instructive examples
and you better understand the list of supported and unsupported features
of the current release.


Features supported
------------------

The parser is a full Ada9X parser, and includes the latest decisions of the
mapping team, including the keyowrd -abstract-. Semantic analysis and code 
generation are in constant evolution, and several important Ada9X features
have been implemented in this release. In particular, tagged types, type
extensions, and dynamic dispatching (but no private extensions yet) as well
as the full hierarchical library mechanism. 
 

