A revised port of Magic 6.3 has been uploaded to tsx-11 and sunsite.  Here is
the top-level "Readme" file:


                          Linux Port #2 of Magic 6.3 
                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

                             by har@caltech.edu

Disclaimer:  Use these programs at your own risk!  Magic is famous for having
             bugs and eccentricities, and I am not responsible for it or your
             chips in any way.

Magic is a graphical CAD tool for generating physical layout descriptions of
VLSI circuits.  The Magic system includes various utilities for conversion of
extracted netlists (ext2sim, ext2spice, etc.) and the switch-level circuit
simulator irsim.  See the comp.lsi.cad newsgroup for more information on other
freely-available VLSI CAD tools (e.g. the Chipmunk CAD suite from Caltech).

This is a port of the 1990 DECWRL/Livermore Magic release (a.k.a. Magic 6.3)
to the Linux operating system.  Since Magic is such a complicated program, it
is difficult to know how successful the port is until it is tested by many
users over time.  All of the problems in port #1 have been fixed (hopefully):

	* the :calma read command now works
	* the :zoom command now works properly
	* extcheck and all ext2whatever programs now work
	* irsim now works

In order to run Magic, set the following environment variables (use the name
of this directory on *your* system instead of "/usr/cad/magic-6.3"):

	CAD_HOME=/usr/cad/magic-6.3
	export CAD_HOME
	PATH=$PATH:$CAD_HOME/bin
	MANPATH=$MANPATH:$CAD_HOME/man

To recompile the Magic programs, irsim, or ext2spice+ (this is the hierarchial
version of ext2spice from ic.berkeley.edu) see the Readme file in the ./src
directory.

Of particular importance will be the step for rebuilding the technology files,
as you will most certainly be tinkering with these even if you don't recompile
the executables.  The current snapshot of the MOSIS scmos technology files
has been included (./src/magic/tech/scmos_mosis).

Feel free to send e-mail to har@caltech.edu regarding problems you think are
due to porting.



