Northern Commander for Linux 0.6 alpha
---------------------------------------

After some alpha testing, there is now a stable version of
Northern Commander for Linux available. NC for Linux is a
filemanager similar to the N***** Commander for DOS, that 
works on every vt100 compatible terminal (including the 
Linux console and xterm). 
NC needs the NCURSES package to compile (BSD curses won't 
do) and part of the documentation is provided in LaTeX format.

Features include:

- Dual-window display with command line as in N***** Commander.
- Mark files with the space bar and execute the usual 
  operations with hotkeys as in N***** Commander.
- All <ESC>-key combinations redefinable with a flexible
  script-based approach.
- Sample scripts for GCC and LaTeX calls provided.
- Fully customizable FTSPL (file type sensitive program 
  launching), shell script based.
- Command line history, customizable prompt.
- Northern Change Directory, a utility for fast directory
  changing, similar to N*****'s NCD.
- Operates with any file viewer and editor, a hex viewer is
  also provided.

Limitations:

- Only VT100 terminals and Linux VT's supported.
- Not every feature you might want is implemented yet ;-)
  e.g. directory tree viewing is not implemented.
- Tested only with "bash". Since NC is heavily shell based,
  porting to other shells requires changes in the sources.
- This is an ALPHA version. It is fairly stable, but there
  might still exist some bugs.

Where to get the new versions:

New versions are first uploaded to ftp.gwdg.de, directory 
pub/linux/misc as nc*.tgz, where * is the version number.
It will also be uploaded to tsx-11 and sunsite.

How to contact the author:

